{"author":"Rebar Bot Nava","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","data":{"alias":"discrepant-comical-horse","creation_channel":"cli","description":"## Observation\n\n`next_batch.compute` builds `ticket_status_map` only from `list_states(status=\"open,in_progress\")` plus tombstones. `_open_blockers_by_ticket` then looks each blocker up with a default of `\"closed\"`. A blocker whose status is `blocked` or `idea` is missing from the map, so it reads as `closed` (terminal), and the ticket it blocks is offered as ready.\n\nReproduced at unit level on db9352e6da0. A is `blocked`; B is `open` and `depends_on` A.\n- With next-batch's status map (`{\"B\": \"open\"}`): `_open_blockers_by_ticket` returns `{}`, so B is ready.\n- With A's real status in the map: it returns `{\"B\": [\"A\"]}`.\n- `is_terminal_status(\"blocked\")` and `is_terminal_status(\"idea\")` are both False.\n\n`ready` (`graph/_ready.py`) gets this right: it checks every blocker's reduced status and treats only missing tickets as finished. So the two ops disagree on whether B is workable.\n\nUnchecked: how many open tickets in the live store currently depend on a `blocked`/`idea` ticket.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] A test where an open task under an epic depends on a `blocked` (and, separately, an `idea`) ticket shows next-batch reporting it as skipped-blocked, not batched.\n- [ ] next-batch takes blocker status from the reduced state of every ticket, not from the open/in_progress list.\n- [ ] The existing next-batch tests and the parity tests still pass.","id":"8e53-5ed2-6791-4308","parent_id":"","priority":2,"tags":[],"ticket_type":"bug","title":"next-batch treats a blocker in blocked or idea status as finished"},"env_id":"492a5c71-6cda-48a6-8134-02ff6a672739","event_type":"CREATE","timestamp":1793291099427456001,"uuid":"7c436316-8f0c-4c95-bb15-7ebe8476b951"}